From 2f3320b0facd89c956ea9f301f8ddd9028c14ac1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 04:41:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] text: Chrome's line breaking for ASCII pairs, overflow-wrap,
 CJK fonts and punctuation trimming
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Line breaking: between two printable ASCII characters Chrome uses Blink's kAsciiLineBreakTable
instead of UAX #14, so it never breaks a URL after `/` and only breaks after `-` and `?` and
before an opening bracket that follows closing punctuation. The table was derived from Chrome
pair by pair and replaces ICU's decision for such pairs (not under break-all or anywhere).

overflow-wrap: anywhere | break-word was never implemented; URLs only wrapped because of the
UAX #14 breaks after `/`. A word that would overflow is now split into glyph clusters, and a
break inside it is taken only when it does not fit on a line of its own; `anywhere` also
contributes those opportunities to the min-content size.

CJK fonts: for ja, zh and ko content Chrome replaces the standard, serif, sans-serif, cursive
and fantasy generics (which have no ideographs) with the language's CJK font, so all the text,
Latin included, uses it. On Linux that is the Noto CJK face for the language; Han text used to
fall back to the Hong Kong face whatever the language. Form controls use Arial, as Chrome's
control font is that named family and does not follow the language.

Punctuation: text-spacing-trim: normal sets one of two adjacent fullwidth punctuation marks
half-width with the font's halt feature (「、。」, （「, 」）, ”，), following Blink's HanKerning:
which marks count as opening, closing or middle comes from the font's glyphs, so centered
Traditional Chinese commas behave unlike left-aligned Japanese ones. Line-start and line-end
trimming are not implemented.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
